From 6d6d551773a2e982c794bce9b7482985ff12e1cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <gianson@pm.me>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 17:30:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/28] Filters: state in registers, invariant branches out of
 the loops (bit-exact)

The ladders and the SVF ran on their members and on jcong directly. The
output store (a q31_t) may alias all of them as far as the compiler knows,
so every sample loaded ~20 values and stored 5-7 again. Now each loop works
on a copy in local variables and writes it back after the block. The
buffers are render buffers (Voice's spareRenderingBuffer, GlobalEffectable's,
FilterSet's tempRenderBuffer), never a filter or jcong, and nothing in the
loops calls out, so this is exactly the same computation.

On top, all exact:
- Branches that depend on the configuration only are taken once per block:
  LP ladder scaleInput's saturation (morph > 0 || resonance > 510M), HP
  ladder's antialiasing / tanh / none, SVF's output mix.
- Morph 0: LP ladder's morph term 2 * ((input * 0) >> 32) is 0; HP ladder's
  cutoff smmlar(fc, input << 4, 0) is fc for every sample.
- SVF: a mix term with coefficient 0 adds exactly 0 (smmlar with a zero
  product adds (0 + 2^31) >> 32 = 0) and goes.
- HP ladder without antialiasing: hpfLastWorkingValue is read by nothing in
  the block, so it's written once, from the last sample.
- Feedback sums with smmlar (sum + smmulr(a, b) exactly, sum << 32 doesn't
  touch the rounding), noisy moveability with smmla (m + smmul(m, n)
  exactly, modulo 2^32 like the add); (a << 2) + (b << 2) as (a + b) << 2.
- sampleIncrement 1 (all callers) gets its own loops, a free register; any
  other increment keeps a per-sample branch as before.
- Stereo: one pass per channel (they are independent). The LP ladder's
  noise draws alternate between the channels, so each pass steps the
  generator twice per sample (69069^2, 69070 * 1234567 mod 2^32), the left
  one starting one step before jcong (inverse of 69069 mod 2^32); the
  oversampled drive ladder keeps its loop over both.
- Parallel routing adds the two buffers with NEON (wraps like add).

Filter bench (ARM, instructions per 128-sample block), all hashes identical
incl. 2048 new randomized cases: LP24 mono 9038 -> 5870, res30 10830 ->
7414, HP ladder 7748 -> 3427, SVF LPF 7869 -> 5717, stereo LP24 17991 ->
12162.

Song (tests/song, demand): 1,370,014 -> 1,173,302 instructions per 128
samples (LpLadderFilter::doFilter 403k -> 271k, HpLadderFilter::doFilter
121k -> 57k). Its measured.wav differs from the v12 baseline's, but only
because of the memory layout: v12 with 17,744 bytes of never-run padding
code (same .data and heap addresses as this build) renders exactly the same
measured.wav as this build.

